================
Disable Unused Ports

Why we did this
================
In many networks, several switch ports remain unused.
By default, if these ports are left active, anyone can physically connect a laptop, PC, router, or access point and gain network access.
This creates a major security risk because attackers or unauthorized users can use these open ports to enter the network.
To reduce the attack surface, administrators disable all unused switch ports.
This is one of the simplest and most effective Layer 2 security best practices.

What will happen
================
1. Unused switch ports are administratively shut down.
2. No device can use these ports.
3. If someone connects a device, no network connectivity is provided.
4. The port remains down until an administrator enables it.
5. The organization's attack surface is reduced.

Example:

Switch Ports:
Fa0/1  -> User PC
Fa0/2  -> IP Phone
Fa0/3  -> Printer
Fa0/4  -> Unused

If Fa0/4 remains active:
- Anyone can connect a device and gain network access.

If Fa0/4 is shutdown:
- Connected device receives no connectivity.

Advantages and Disadvantages of this, hence what came next
==========================================================
Advantages
----------
1. Prevents unauthorized physical access.
2. Reduces attack surface.
3. Easy to implement.
4. No additional hardware required.
5. Recommended security best practice.
6. Prevents accidental device connections.

Disadvantages
-------------
1. Manual administration required.
2. Ports must be enabled when new devices are added.
3. Does not protect active ports.
4. Provides only physical access protection.
5. Requires proper documentation.

Hence what came next
--------------------
Disabling unused ports is good, but attackers may still connect to an active port.
Administrators wanted a method to isolate and trap unauthorized devices even when they connect to a switch port.

This led to:
Black Hole VLAN
Instead of simply leaving ports unused, they can be assigned to an unused VLAN with no Layer 3 connectivity, preventing access to production networks.

One-Shot Configuration
======================

Switch(config)# interface range fa0/4 - 24
Switch(config-if-range)# shutdown
Switch(config-if-range)# description UNUSED_PORT
Switch(config-if-range)# end

Verification
============

show interfaces status

show running-config

Expected Result
===============

Unused Ports:
-------------
Administratively Down

Connected Device:
-----------------
No Connectivity

Network Security:
-----------------
Improved

Summary
=======

Problem:
Unused switch ports can be used by unauthorized users to gain network access.

Solution:
Administratively shut down all unused ports.

Benefit:
Reduces physical attack vectors and strengthens Layer 2 security.

Limitation:
Only protects unused ports. Active ports still require additional security controls.

